/**
@page page_exagrype_fo_so_ccz4 First-order second-order CCZ4

\tableofcontents

This is a brief sketch of the first-order second-order hybrid CCZ4 implementation in ExaGRyPE.

The first-order CCZ4 solver (exagrype.api.FOCCZ4Solver) evolves 59 unknowns.
This is the original Dumbser/Zanotti formulation introduced with ExaHyPE 2 and 
is a "clean" first-order formulation of the CCZ4 problem. A first-order 
formulation is not what you find in most codes in the field. Most codes start
to discretise the genuine second-order formulation of the field equations 
straightaway. The reformulation into first order was driven, in parts, by 
ExaHyPE's need to phrase all equations in first order, such that a generic
first-order mathematical toolset can be thrown at the problem.


In this first-order formulation, 
34 out of the 59 unknowns are first derivatives of the other 25 (lapse, shift, conformal
metric, phi), plus K0. So they are helper variables which now evolve in time.
In princple however, they can always be reconstructed from the 25 dominant 
variables. To the best of our knowledge, it is neither clear if this first-order
formulation is faster nor if it is as stable as a second order approach. 
While ExaHyPE does not support second-order formulations, ExaGRyPE provides 
a third variant, which is kind of a hybrid fo first- and second-order
formulation: A FOSO CCZ4 implementation! It is a reconstructed first-order/second-order
scheme.


In the reconstructed formulation, we only
evolve the 25 primary unknowns and recompute the derivatives from them
explicitly after each time step: The evolution and ocmpute kernels of 
CCZ4 still feed only from first order derivatives, but these derivatives
are constructed explicitly from the solution prior to the kernel application. 

We realise this with two solvers which take turns in the time stepping task
graph. They are hosted by one exagrype.api.FOSOSolver, the only object a user
has to create and add to the project:

| | Evolved ("real") unknowns | Auxiliary variables |
|---|---|---|
| exagrype.api.FOSOCCZ4Solver (FOSOSolver.ccz4) | 25 primary variables | 34 derivatives |
| exagrype.api.FOSOReconstructionSolver (FOSOSolver.reconstruction) | 34 derivatives | 25 primary variables |

Both see the same 59 entries, but with swapped roles.

- The reconstruction solver runs first. A task of it invokes the reconstruction of the
  quantities that the first-order CCZ4 solver requires (exagrype::ccz4::recomputeAuxiliaryVariablesFD4_4thOrder()).
  It computes these derivatives from the primary variables with halo. The derivatives
  are then projected onto the faces.
- The solver finds the derivatives with a halo, reconstructs its own 25
  unknowns with a halo, and calls the very same CCZ4 kernels as
  the first-order solver. As the kernels are the second-order variants, they only
  evolve the first 25 unknowns.
- CCZ4 code must not be duplicated. All CCZ4 kernels, parameters, initial
  conditions and the constraint enforcement are shared between the first-order
  and the second-order solver.
- Eventually, SO has to give results very similar to the first-order solver. The
  difference is to be quantified by benchmarks (@ref benchmarks_exagrype_fo_vs_so).
- Both solvers exist for Finite Volumes (FV) and fourth-order Finite
  Differences (FD4). They differ in their  reconstruction: FD4 uses a fourth-order stencil
  in each Runge-Kutta stage, FV uses second-order central differences once per
  time step (see below).



<div style="background-color: #cdc ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;">
 The following text is easier to digest once you read the description on 
 @ref page_exahype_programmers_guide_coupling and notably the section on 
 multiplicative coupling there.
</div>


# Design

## Class structure

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
exahype2.api.Solver
  ├── exagrype.api.CCZ4SolverBase     parameters, PDE terms (via hooks), initial
  │     │                              condition, constraint enforcement
  │     ├── exagrype.api.FOCCZ4Solver     59 unknowns, secondary pointer is Q+25
  │     └── exagrype.api.FOSOCCZ4Solver   25 unknowns + auxiliary variables "Derivatives" (34),
  │                                        secondary pointer is "derivatives"
  └── exagrype.api.FOSOReconstructionSolver   34 unknowns (the derivatives), no PDE terms

exagrype.api.FOSOSolver                 hosts one FOSOCCZ4Solver (.ccz4) and one
                                         FOSOReconstructionSolver (.reconstruction)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The pair follows the recipe of the multiplicative coupling in
@ref page_exahype_programmers_guide_coupling, repeated per Runge-Kutta stage.
The reconstruction solver is a real solver (it has a C++ class and an instance in
the solver repository), but a very lean one: it has no PDE terms, no compute kernel, no
lopsided advection, no constraint enforcement, no grid construction, no plotter and it
does not compute a time step size. Its C++ class forwards the time stamps and time step sizes
of the CCZ4 solver, so the global minimum over all solvers is not spoilt.

The kernels in exagrype::ccz4 always work on 59 entries, which they take as two
pointers: one to the 25 primary entries and one to the 34 secondary entries. The
base class generates the PDE term implementations once. The only thing that
differs is a tiny hook, which tells the generated code where the secondary
entries live:

- FOCCZ4Solver: behind the primary ones, i.e. the pointers are Q and Q+25.
- FOSOCCZ4Solver: in the auxiliary variables, i.e. the pointers are Q and
  "derivatives". Nothing is copied. As the solver does not evolve the secondary
  entries, it passes a nullptr for the secondary part of the result, and the
  kernels skip it.

The same holds for the initial condition: any routine from
exagrype.api.initialvalues fills 59 entries; the SO solver wraps it, runs it on a
local array and splits the outcome over Q and the derivatives. Therefore, the
initial conditions are not duplicated either.
The constraint enforcement of the SO solver uses
exagrype::ccz4::enforceCCZ4constraintsOfEvolvedVariables(), which is the first
part of exagrype::ccz4::enforceCCZ4constraints() (it is called by the latter, i.e.
the code is shared).

## Data

The derivatives are an instance of exahype2.api.AuxiliaryVariables (named
"Derivatives", the data is called "DerivativeVariables"), owned by the SO solver. It
sits on the faces and can change. This re-uses all the existing machinery:
a cell patch, face overlaps, halo reconstruction in the solver's task graph.
Before this project, auxiliary variables were written once at the setup. The
reconstruction solver is the first component which updates them in every time step.
They are its unknowns, i.e. it operates on exactly the patch of the auxiliary variables,
and nothing is copied.

## Task graph

Both solvers build a task graph of their own (and report it), and the host
(exagrype.api.FOSOSolver) concatenates them and reports the outcome.
The graph of the reconstruction solver is one chain of tasks per Runge-Kutta stage
\f$ s \f$ (one chain for FV). The chains are not connected to each other.
The host makes the first tasks of each chain take over the dependencies of the reconstruction task of the CCZ4 solver,
and lets the latter wait for the last task of the chain:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
Project stage s onto faces (SO)
     │
     ├──────────────────────────────────────┐
     ▼                                      ▼
Reconstruct primary variables s      Reconstruct derivative variables s     (SOReconstruction)
     ▼                                      ▼
Interpolate primary variables s      Interpolate derivative variables s
     └──────────────────┬───────────────────┘
                        ▼
              Reconstruct derivatives s       (exagrype::ccz4::reconstructFirstDerivativesFD4())
                        ▼
        Project derivative variables onto faces s
                        ▼
        Restrict derivative variables on hanging faces s   (only if CHANGING_ON_FACES)
                        ▼
        Roll over derivative variable faces s
                        ▼
Reconstruct stage s (SO)  ->  Evaluate right-hand side s (SO)  ->  Prepare stage s+1 (SO)  ->  ...
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

From the point of view of the reconstruction solver, the 25 primary variables are auxiliary variables
and the derivatives are its unknowns. Both sit on the faces, i.e. we reconstruct the halos of both and interpolate
both onto hanging faces. Only the derivatives are projected, restricted and rolled over, as only they change.
Restricting depends on the mode of the derivatives (@ref exahype2.api.AuxiliaryVariables.Mode), which is
CHANGING_ON_FACES here, while general auxiliary variables default to STATIONARY_ON_FACES.
The projection, the restriction and the roll over share the temporary update patch of the face data.
They therefore have to end up in one mesh sweep. As the task graph compiler does not fuse tasks with
conflicting face data, the projection declares that it writes the face data: it starts a new sweep that way.

The host realises the CCZ4 solver first, as the reconstruction solver needs its data. The CCZ4 solver
owns the global tasks (load balancing switch, dynamic mesh refinement), see @ref page_exahype_programmers_guide_coupling.
The task graph compiler fuses
the tasks as usual, i.e. the reconstruction of the derivatives and the solver's
own reconstruction typically end up in neighbouring (producer/consumer) mesh
sweeps.

## FD4 specifics

The routine recomputeAuxiliaryVariablesFD4_4thOrder() uses the stencil
\f$ [1\ -8\ 0\ 8\ -1]/(12h) \f$ and needs a halo of three. The solver evaluates
the fluxes and non-conservative products on the faces of a cell, i.e. it needs
the derivatives left and right of each face. The derivatives are thus required
one layer outside of the cell, and the stencil reaches two layers further out.
The plain FD4 scheme of ExaHyPE only has a halo of two. exagrype.api.FOSOCCZ4Solver therefore
requires a halo of at least three: exagrype.api.FOSOReconstructionSolver rejects
numerical schemes with a smaller halo. A halo of three is given by
exagrype.api.FD4WithKODissipation and by exahype2.api.rkfd4.CentralDifferencesWithRungeKutta
with lopsided advection (as used by the benchmark). The wrapper
exagrype::ccz4::reconstructFirstDerivativesFD4() hands the halo straight on to
the routine.


## FV specifics

The Finite Volume variant (exahype2.api.fv.RusanovWithExplicitEuler, overlap one) reuses the
reconstruction solver with three differences:

- Stencil: exagrype::ccz4::reconstructFirstDerivativesCentralDifferences() applies
  \f$ [-1\ 0\ 1]/(2h) \f$. It reaches one layer out, i.e. the overlap of one is
  enough. The derivatives one layer outside of the cell (needed for the
  Rusanov flux and the non-conservative product on the faces) are not computed from a
  wider halo but by the neighbour cell and exchanged via the faces. The scheme
  is second-order accurate in the derivatives.
- One chain: there is a single stage, so the reconstruction solver inserts a single chain of tasks
  in front of the solver's "Reconstruct" task, and
  uses the Finite Volume action sets for the projection onto the faces.
- Stateless PDE terms: the Finite Volume kernels call the PDE terms as static
  functions. exagrype.api.CCZ4SolverBase therefore registers stateless
  non-conservative product, source and eigenvalue terms. The kernels only use
  compile-time constants, so this loses nothing. The Finite Differences
  (exahype2.api.rkfd4) call stateless terms, too (they forward a dummy
  exahype2::Solver::Offloadable tag to the static method), i.e. there is no case
  distinction between the schemes. Sommerfeld boundary conditions would
  need a stateful eigenvalue term (exahype2.api.blockstructured.BoundaryConditions),
  but the CCZ4 solvers have not been used with them yet.


## Explanation of the need of an "additional" boundary layer and the modelling of the reconstruction as real solver

Add the heart of the FOSO CCZ4 formalism is still a first-order update of the primary 
unknowns with standard first-order techniques. For finite volumes, we want, for example,
to reconstruct the ncp (non-conservative product) on a face to feed into the numerical 
scheme (step 1). For this reconstruction, we need the ncp value in the left and right 
adjacent cell. Within these cells, we can calculate this value, as we know the solution 
- which is piecewise constant for our simple FV scheme.

@image html documentation/exagrype/so-reconstruction-overlaps.png

For this "final" compute step, we need a halo of one, and we have to exchange face data
prior to the time step. Into the ncp calculation in the left neighbour feed the 
auxiliary variables, which are effectively placeholders for the derivatives of 
the solution. If we want to reconstruct these guys, we again need a left and right 
neighbour, and these neighbour accesses again exceed the local patch.

Therefore, employing a FOSO scheme over FV requires a halo size of at least 2, and there
has to be an additional data exchange step. Otherwise, the reconstruction would fail. 
For FD4, the halo has to be at least 3. 


# Lab notes

## What worked

- **Auxiliary variables as the data model.** The derivatives could be modelled
  as exahype2.api.AuxiliaryVariables without any change to ExaHyPE. The rkfd4 scheme
  already hands the solver's auxiliary variables to the kernels in the same
  array as the unknowns (unknowns first, then the auxiliary variables), which
  is exactly the layout of the 59 CCZ4 entries. Evolving only 25 of them needs
  nothing besides the existing "copy only the unknowns back" logic.
- **Separate graphs, concatenated by the host.** Initially, the reconstruction solver
  looked up the tasks of the CCZ4 solver by name in the one time stepping graph that the
  project shares among all solvers, and added itself as an additional dependency. That entangled the
  two solvers, and the user saw only one half-finished graph after the other. Now, each
  solver builds a graph of its own, and exahype2.api.SolverHost concatenates them and
  lets exagrype.api.FOSOSolver.couple() add the dependencies. The price is a flag
  (add_global_tasks) that all schemes honour, so the load balancing switch and the dynamic
  mesh refinement exist once.
- **Functor over the reconstructed halos.** exagrype.api.actionsets.ApplyReconstructionFunctor runs a
  user-supplied snippet over the halos that the preceding tasks have filled. The snippet is one call
  into the C++ library, i.e. the generated code stays tiny.
- **Refactoring FOCCZ4Solver.** After moving the logic into the base class,
  the unit tests of the first-order solver (tests/unit/test_exagrype_ccz4_solver.py,
  test_exagrype_fd4_with_ko_dissipation.py) passed unchanged.

## What did not work (initially), and how we fixed it

- **The reconstruction as a second exahype2.api.Solver / project member.**
  The first implementation hid the reconstruction in an object that the CCZ4 solver
  created and realised itself, because the project's repository generator expects
  every solver to have a C++ solver class and a global instance
  (get_name_of_solver_class() etc.), and the reconstruction has no PDE terms and
  no time stepping. The design therefore did not mirror the coupling
  concept. Now, exagrype.api.FOSOReconstructionSolver is a real solver. The
  missing time stepping is not a problem: a minimal numerical scheme only
  inserts the reconstruction tasks, and the C++ class forwards the time step
  data of the CCZ4 solver. Otherwise, the global minimum of the time step sizes
  over all solvers, which the repository evaluates, would be that of a
  solver that never time steps.
- **Peano's stacks and the face data.** The first implementation declared the
  faces of the derivatives as WRITE in the reconstruction's task. In a debug
  build, Peano aborted in the first time step in
  SpacetreeSet::exchangeAllPeriodicBoundaryDataStacks() with
  "stackContainer.getForPush(...)->empty()" (a stack of 81 entries was not
  empty). Rule of thumb: every face (or cell) datum that has been written
  once has to be loaded (read) by the next task that touches it, as otherwise
  the entries pile up on Peano's stacks. The setup writes the derivatives'
  faces, therefore the reconstruction has to declare them READ_AND_WRITE
  (it loads the old value, the roll-over overwrites it). The stage input
  needs READ_AND_WRITE for the same reason, as the solver's reconstruction
  reads it afterwards.
- **Two rkfd4 solvers in one project.** Both solvers write the solver-independent
  kernel kernels/rungekutta_linear_combination.cpph, and the generator appends to
  the file. The second solver therefore duplicates the function
  (redefinition error). The same happens if you re-run a generator in an
  already populated directory (also the solver-specific kernels are duplicated
  then). The benchmark works around it by building FO and SO in separate
  directories, and deleting the directory first. A proper fix, i.e.
  making exahype2.api.rungekutta.generate_linear_combination_kernel()
  idempotent, is outside of this project's scope and left to a decision of the
  maintainers.
- **enforceCCZ4constraints() on 25 unknowns.** The routine touches entries up to
  index 54 and would read/write beyond the 25 unknowns of the SO patch. We
  split it (see above). The first-order solver's behaviour is bit-identical.
- **Logging.** With LoggerVerbosity.TEXT, debug builds log every data movement
  (several hundred thousand lines per second). A benchmark that runs for a
  few time steps then takes forever. For tests use LoggerVerbosity.OFF and the
  release build.

*/
